{"name":"Dovetail","hex":"#908a83","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"dovetail","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The tail of a dove (family Columbidae); also, something having the shape of a dove's tail.","In full dovetail joint: a type of joint where adjoining components are fastened by multiple tenons cut into wedge shapes resembling a dove's tail, which interlock with mortises having corresponding shapes."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To unite (components) with a dovetail (noun sense 2.1) or similar joint.\n\n\nTo combine or fit (things) together well.","To unite (components) with a dovetail (noun sense 2.1) or similar joint."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dovetail"}],"entries":[{"title":"Dovetail (company)","blocks":[{"text":"Dovetail is an Australian software company. Users create tags to perform transcription analysis and coding interpretation of interviews, survey responses and feedback, and create summarized insights from their research analysis."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dovetail_(company)"},{"title":"Dovetail (restaurant)","blocks":[{"text":"Dovetail was a restaurant in New York City. John Fraser was the chef and owner, until he sold the business to partners in 2018. Established in 2007, the restaurant served American cuisine and had received a Michelin star."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dovetail_(restaurant)"},{"title":"Dovetail joint","blocks":[{"text":"A dovetail joint is a joinery technique most commonly used in woodworking joinery (carpentry), including furniture, cabinets, log buildings, and traditional timber framing. Noted for its resistance to being pulled apart, also known as tensile strength, the dovetail joint is commonly used to join the sides of a drawer to the front. A series of pins cut to extend from the end of one board interlock with a series of 'tails' cut into the end of another board. The pins and tails have a trapezoidal shape. Once glued, a wooden dovetail joint requires no mechanical fasteners."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Finished_dovetail.jpg/330px-Finished_dovetail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dovetail_joint"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Gersa18.jpg/500px-Gersa18.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Gersa18.jpg/1280px-Gersa18.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Gersa18.jpg/500px-Gersa18.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Dovetailed woodworking joints on a Romanian church","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGersa18.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Perumthachan_Pillar%E0%B4%B5%E0%B4%BE%E0%B4%B4%E0%B4%AA%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%AA%E0%B4%B3%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%B3%E0%B4%BF.jpg/500px-Perumthachan_Pillar%E0%B4%B5%E0%B4%BE%E0%B4%B4%E0%B4%AA%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%AA%E0%B4%B3%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%B3%E0%B4%BF.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Perumthachan_Pillar%E0%B4%B5%E0%B4%BE%E0%B4%B4%E0%B4%AA%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%AA%E0%B4%B3%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%B3%E0%B4%BF.jpg/500px-Perumthachan_Pillar%E0%B4%B5%E0%B4%BE%E0%B4%B4%E0%B4%AA%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%AA%E0%B4%B3%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%B3%E0%B4%BF.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Stone pillar at the Vazhappally Maha Siva Temple","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APerumthachan_Pillar%E0%B4%B5%E0%B4%BE%E0%B4%B4%E0%B4%AA%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%AA%E0%B4%B3%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%B3%E0%B4%BF.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Joinery-halfblinddovetail.gif/330px-Joinery-halfbli